.TH CPIO 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
cpio \- create, list, and extract newc cpio archives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpio \-o \-H newc
.RB [ \-0L ]
.RB [ \-F
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-R
.IR owner ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B cpio \-i
.RB [ \-dmuv ]
.RB [ \-F
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-R
.IR owner ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ pattern ...]
.br
.B cpio \-t
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-F
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-R
.IR owner ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ pattern ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cpio
creates, lists, and extracts archives in the SVR4 cpio format with ASCII
headers, known as
.BR newc .
This is the busybox
.BR cpio .
Its three modes are chosen by
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.BR \-t :
.TP
.B \-o
Create an archive.
It reads a list of file names from standard input and writes the archive
to standard output.
.TP
.B \-i
Extract the files in an archive read from standard input.
.TP
.B \-t
List the entries in an archive read from standard input.
.PP
.B \-F
names a file to use instead of standard input or output.
Archives are never compressed or decompressed, so a compressed archive
goes through a program such as
.BR gzip (1)
or
.BR zcat (1).
.SS Creating
Each line of standard input names one file, and a CR before the LF is
removed.
With
.BR \-0 ,
names end with NUL bytes instead, and nothing else is removed.
Empty names are skipped, and leading
.B ./
components are removed.
Other names are stored as given, with backslashes, drive letters, and
.B ..\&
components unchanged.
.PP
A directory is stored as an entry of its own, without its contents, so
the list usually comes from
.BR find (1).
A symbolic link is stored as a link, unless
.B \-L
is given.
.PP
Each entry holds the file's name, type, permissions, owner, inode number,
link count, modification time, and size, followed by its contents, or for
a symbolic link, the link's target.
The names of a file with a link count above 1 are held back and written
at the end of the archive in reverse order, with the contents only on the
last one, which is the name that was listed first.
The archive ends with an entry named
.BR TRAILER!!! ,
and is not padded to a multiple of 512 bytes.
.PP
A name that does not exist or cannot be read, a file of 4 GiB or more,
or a file that becomes shorter while it is read ends the command with an
error.
The archive is then incomplete and has no trailer.
.SS Reading
.B \-i
and
.B \-t
read entries up to the
.B TRAILER!!!\&
entry and ignore anything after it.
An archive that ends between entries without a trailer is accepted.
They read
.B newc
headers, which begin with
.BR 070701 ,
and
.B crc
headers, which begin with
.BR 070702 ,
without checking the checksums.
Other cpio formats are rejected.
Leading slashes are removed from each name.
.PP
With
.I pattern
operands, only the entries whose names match at least one pattern are
extracted or listed.
A pattern that matches nothing is not an error.
A pattern is a shell wildcard that must match the whole name.
It is case-sensitive.
.B *
and
.B ?\&
also match
.B /
and a leading period.
A backslash quotes the next character, so a backslash in a name must be
doubled in a pattern.
.SS Listing
.B \-t
prints the name of each entry on a line of its own.
With
.BR \-v ,
each line holds the permissions in the form used by
.BR "ls \-l" ,
the user and group IDs separated by a slash, the size right-aligned in 9
columns, the modification time as local time in the form
.IR "YYYY\-MM\-DD hh:mm:ss" ,
and the name.
A symbolic link is shown with a size of 0, and its name is followed by
.B \->
and the target.
.PP
After the trailer, both
.B \-t
and
.B \-i
print the size of the archive on standard error as
.IB n " blocks\fR,"
counting 512-byte blocks and rounding up.
Nothing is printed for an archive with no trailer.
.SS Extracting
Each entry is created under its name, relative to the current
directory.
When a file of that name exists and its modification time is the same as
or later than the entry's, the file is kept, and for an entry that is not
a directory the message
.IB name " not created: newer or same age file exists"
is printed.
Otherwise the file is removed and replaced.
With
.BR \-u ,
it is removed regardless of its age, unless the entry is a directory,
which then leaves it in place.
Failing to remove a file ends the command.
.PP
By type:
.IP \(bu 2
A regular file is created with the archived contents.
.IP \(bu 2
A directory is created.
An error creating it is reported without ending the command.
.IP \(bu 2
A symbolic link is created as described in
.BR NOTES .
.IP \(bu 2
A hard link, a regular file entry with a link count above 1 and no
contents, is created after all other entries, as a link to the entry
with the same inode number that has the contents, or as an empty file if
there is none.
It fails if a
.I pattern
leaves out the entry with the contents.
.IP \(bu 2
A device node, FIFO, or socket cannot be created on Windows, and is
reported and skipped.
.PP
The parent directory of each entry must exist, unless
.B \-d
is given.
New files and directories get the current time, unless
.B \-m
is given.
The archived owner is ignored.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-idv ,
and may follow the
.I pattern
operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-Fsrc.cpio ,
or be the next argument.
Long options may be abbreviated to a unique prefix, and take their
argument after
.B =
or as the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
One of
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-o ,
and
.B \-t
is required, or the usage message is printed.
If several are given,
.B \-o
overrides the others, and
.B \-t
overrides
.BR \-i .
Options that do not apply to the mode are ignored.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-list
List the entries.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-extract
Extract the entries.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-create
Create an archive.
Requires
.BR "\-H newc" .
.TP
\fB\-H\fR \fIformat\fR, \fB\-\-format=\fR\fIformat\fR
Set the archive format for
.BR \-o .
Only
.B newc
is supported, and any
.I format
that begins with
.B n
is taken as
.BR newc .
Without
.BR \-o ,
the format is ignored, since
.B newc
and
.B crc
archives are recognized by their headers.
.TP
.B \-d
With
.BR \-i ,
create the missing parent directories of each entry.
Only
.B /
separates directory names here.
.TP
.B \-m
With
.BR \-i ,
set the access and modification times of each extracted file and
directory, but not symbolic link, to the archived modification time.
A directory's times change again when files are extracted into it later.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
With
.BR \-t ,
print a long listing.
With
.BR \-i ,
print the name of each entry on standard output, or on standard error
with
.BR \-\-to\-stdout ,
after processing it, including entries not created because a newer file
exists, but not hard links without contents.
Names are printed in the form of a plain
.B \-t
listing.
With
.BR \-o ,
it has no effect.
.TP
.B \-u
With
.BR \-i ,
replace existing files regardless of their age.
.TP
\fB\-F\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-file=\fR\fIfile\fR
With
.B \-t
or
.BR \-i ,
read the archive from
.I file
instead of standard input.
With
.BR \-o ,
create or truncate
.I file
and write the archive to it instead of standard output.
.TP
\fB\-R\fR \fIowner\fR, \fB\-\-owner=\fR\fIowner\fR
With
.BR \-o ,
store
.I owner
in place of each file's owner.
With
.B \-t
or
.BR \-i ,
use it in place of each entry's owner, which matters only for the IDs
that
.B \-tv
shows.
.I owner
is
.IR user ,
which sets the user ID,
.BI : group\fR,
which sets the group ID,
.IB user :\fR,
which sets both to the user's ID, or
.IB user : group\fR.
A
.I user
is a decimal ID,
.BR root ,
which is 0, or the name of the current user, which is 4095.
A
.I group
must be a decimal ID.
Any other name is an error.
.TP
.B \-L
With
.BR \-o ,
store the file that a symbolic link or junction refers to, instead of
the link.
.TP
.BR \-0 ", " \-\-null
With
.BR \-o ,
read names that end with NUL bytes, as written by
.BR "find \-print0" ,
instead of lines.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-devno
With
.BR \-o ,
store device numbers as 0.
Device numbers are always 0 on Windows, so this has no effect.
.TP
.B \-\-renumber\-inodes
With
.BR \-o ,
store inode numbers counting from 1 in the order the files are first
named in the list, instead of the files' own.
The names of a hard-linked file share one number.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Do not print the block count.
.TP
.B \-\-to\-stdout
With
.BR \-i ,
write the contents of each matching entry to standard output, one after
another, instead of creating anything.
With
.BR \-v ,
the names go to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit.
The message shows the short options and only the long options
.B \-\-ignore\-devno
and
.BR \-\-renumber\-inodes .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the command line are expanded at startup into matching file
names, even if quoted, for callers such as
.B cmd.exe
that do not expand them, so a
.I pattern
such as
.B *.c
may become a list of file names.
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
patterns as the shell passed them.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
An octal file mode creation mask applied at startup.
The default is
.BR 0002 .
With
.BR \-o ,
its group and other write bits are removed from the stored permissions.
If it includes the owner write bit,
.BR 0200 ,
the
.I file
created by
.B \-F
is read-only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must precede the
.I pattern
operands.
.TP
.B TZ
The time zone of the times printed by
.BR "\-t \-v" .
The format is that of the Microsoft C runtime, such as
.BR EST5EDT .
When it is unset, the Windows time zone setting applies.
.PP
As for all busybox applets, critical-error dialogs depend on
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
and output to a console depends on
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.BR LANG ;
see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including when some entries were reported and not created: a
directory that could not be made, a device node, FIFO, or socket, or a
file kept because a newer one exists.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line, reported with a usage message on standard
error, an unknown
.B \-R
user or group, or an error that ends the command: a damaged or truncated
archive, or a file or link that could not be read, created, or removed.
.PP
Errors are reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
cpio: \fImessage\fR
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Archive a directory tree:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find src | cpio \-o \-H newc >src.cpio
.EE
.RE
.PP
List it in detail:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cpio \-tv <src.cpio
drwxrwxr\-x 4095/4095         0 2026\-10\-19 20:43:04 src
\-rw\-rw\-r\-\- 4095/4095        29 2026\-10\-19 20:43:04 src/main.c
\-rw\-rw\-r\-\- 4095/4095        13 2026\-10\-19 20:43:04 src/Makefile
2 blocks
.EE
.RE
.PP
Extract only the C sources, creating their directories.
The quotes keep the shell from expanding the pattern:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cpio \-id \(aq*.c\(aq <src.cpio
.EE
.RE
.PP
Archive names that may contain newlines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find src \-print0 | cpio \-o0 \-H newc >src.cpio
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy a directory tree into an existing directory, replacing files that
are already there.
This stands in for the pass-through mode:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find src | cpio \-o \-H newc | (cd dest && cpio \-idu)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a compressed Linux initramfs image whose files belong to root:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cd rootfs
$ find . | cpio \-o \-H newc \-R 0:0 | gzip >../initramfs.gz
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print one file from that image:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ zcat initramfs.gz | cpio \-i \-\-to\-stdout \-\-quiet init
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR cpio :
.IP \(bu 2
Only
.B newc
archives are written, and
.B "\-H newc"
is required.
Only
.B newc
and
.B crc
archives are read.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no pass-through mode,
.BR \-p ,
and options not described above, such as
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-B ,
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-r ,
are invalid.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-o
prints no block count, and does not pad the archive to a multiple of 512
bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-R
accepts only a colon between
.I user
and
.IR group ,
not a period.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-t
and
.B \-v
do not list the names of a hard-linked file that have no contents.
.PP
Without
.BR \-u ,
extracting a directory entry where an older directory exists fails with
.BR "can't remove old file" ,
since removing a directory this way fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
This includes the
.B .\&
entry written by
.BR "find ." ,
when the current directory is older.
.B \-u
avoids it.
.PP
Names are stored and extracted byte for byte: UTF-8 on Windows 10
version 1903 and later, and the system code page on earlier versions.
Backslashes are not converted to slashes.
Names are not checked when extracting.
A name with a drive letter, such as
.IR C:/x ,
a leading backslash, or
.B ..\&
components is extracted at that location, even outside the current
directory, so list an archive from an untrusted source with
.B \-t
first.
A name that is not a valid Windows file name, or a path longer than
.BR MAX_PATH ,
260 characters, fails like any other directory or file that cannot be
created.
.PP
Listings, including those of
.BR \-v ,
show each control character as
.BR ? ,
and on Windows 10 version 1903 and later, each byte of a non-ASCII
character as well.
.PP
With
.BR \-o ,
the stored attributes come from busybox-w32's emulation of Unix file
information:
.IP \(bu 2
Everyone may read, and write unless the file is read-only, less the
group and other write bits of the umask.
Directories, and files whose names end in
.BR .bat ,
.BR .cmd ,
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
or
.BR .sh ,
or that begin with
.B #!\&
or are PE executables other than DLLs, are executable.
.IP \(bu 2
A file owned by the current user has user and group ID 4095.
For a file of another owner the ID is 0, or the relative ID of the
owner's account when that is from 500 to 4094.
.IP \(bu 2
Device numbers are 0.
Inode numbers are the low 32 bits of the file index.
Hard links are recognized by the whole file index alone, whatever the
volume.
.IP \(bu 2
Symbolic link targets are stored with slashes for backslashes.
Junctions are stored as symbolic links to their absolute targets, such
as
.IR C:/dir .
.IP \(bu 2
Junctions and symbolic links to directories have a link count of 2, so
they are written at the end of the archive, as hard-linked files are.
.IP \(bu 2
Modification times are stored in 32 bits, so only times from 1970 to
2106 are kept.
.PP
When extracting, the owner write permission alone has an effect: a file
without it is made read-only.
Directories are never read-only, and nothing is made executable.
A hard link needs a file system that supports them, such as NTFS.
A symbolic link is created as a Windows symbolic link, which requires
Developer Mode or administrator rights, with backslashes for slashes in
its target.
It is a directory link only if its target is an existing directory when
the link is made.
A link whose target begins with
.B /
or contains
.BR .. ,
or that cannot be made at once, is made after all other entries, and
failing then ends the command with
.BR "can't create symlink" .
A link made after all other entries, symbolic or hard, is not made when
a directory in its own name is missing, is a symbolic link or junction,
or is not a directory: that fails with
.BR "Not a directory" .
.PP
Standard input and output are in binary mode, so archives pass through
unchanged.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR tar (1),
.BR zcat (1)
